window.docContents[165].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Hypotheses-carried-by-each-solution-concept":{"contents":"Classical transport and wave statements carry differentiability, support,\nand initial-data hypotheses. Local energy is integrated over moving spatial\nregions, so finite propagation depends on the geometry of those regions as\nmuch as on the PDE identity.\n\nNonlinear first-order equations use characteristic ODEs for the position, the\nunknown, and the gradient, and a noncharacteristic condition is what makes the\ncharacteristic coordinates locally invertible. Conservation laws work\ndifferently, with distributional identities, entropy pairs, Rankine–Hugoniot\nrelations, and strong local initial traces.\n\nWeak wave equations live in a Gelfand triple with second-order time\ndynamics. Symmetric first-order systems carry matrix symmetry, coercivity,\nand coefficient regularity. Hamilton–Jacobi statements keep the classical,\nvariational, and viscosity notions apart rather than treating them as\nsynonyms.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.2. Hypotheses carried by each solution concept","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Hypotheses-carried-by-each-solution-concept"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--What-is-known":{"contents":"The obstruction is peculiar to three dimensions. In two dimensions,\nenergy and enstrophy estimates yield global regularity for smooth\nincompressible data. In three dimensions the known theory separates into\nseveral levels.\n\n*  constructed global finite-energy weak\n  solutions. They satisfy an energy inequality, but their global smoothness\n  and uniqueness are not known. The Leray–Hopf and classical solution classes\n  of the Navier–Stokes section make the same distinction.*  proved partial\n  regularity for suitable weak solutions: the possible singular set has\n  one-dimensional parabolic Hausdorff measure zero. Partial regularity does\n  not show that this set is empty.* The Prodi–Serrin family of criteria, represented here by\n  , shows that a weak solution is regular when\n  its velocity belongs locally to  with established the\n  scale-critical endpoint . These results reduce regularity\n  to control of a critical norm; the energy inequality alone controls a\n  weaker scale.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem\u0009The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","header":"8.7.1.2. What is known","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--What-is-known"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.8. Coverage and Open Problems","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems"}});